                               THE BRAID



soaked and dried and soaked again. A woman’s breath, fast. The knock
of wood on wood and the heavy slap of water. A boom from some‐
where I could feel and not hear: a drum, played down into the river,
asking, not taking.
   And on the bank, getting small, a man on a stump with a book
across his knee. The pen, moving.
   I felt the stone notice the pen.
   She noticed it. She didn’t hate it. She registered it, and her jaw set.
   Then the table was a table, and my hand was on stone, and I was
breathing hard.
   That is where the first page of this came from. The ledger was only
the proof. I told you at the start about a woman in a canoe and a man
with a book, and I told it like I’d been there, because I had, once, for
about a minute, through her.
   “Te la llevas,” said my grandmother.
   You’re taking her.
   “Abuela, no. She protects you. She protects the house, the whole
block. You said when she’s awake that thing doesn’t get in.”
   “Tengo setenta y nueve años y un río.” I’m seventy-nine, and I have
a river. “Me las arreglo.” I’ll manage.
   “Abuela—”
   “Ellos tienen fecha.” She said it without heat. “Siete cartas, un par‐
lante en cada colmado, un hombre con un cuaderno. They have a date.
You have a date.” She put a palm on the stone. “They count. So you
must remember in a place where it counts. Not here. There. Where
they’re going to do it.”
   I said nothing. She let it sit.
   “And her son?” I said, finally.
   A long breath.
   “Tu prima Yanaisa lo guarda. Tu prima. La hija de mi sobrina.” She
almost smiled. “Another one with a hard head. She stays. When the day
comes that somebody needs to answer, she crosses.”
   “When the day comes.”
   “You will know. You won’t need to be told.”
   “And when they meet?” I said. “The two of them.”



